      "line_id": "l0005",
      "text": "d) None of the above.",
      "bbox": [
        72.3,
        59.4,
        175.2,
        70.2
      ]
    },
    {
      "line_id": "l0006",
      "text": "CORRECT ANSWER : OPTION A",
      "bbox": [
        72.6,
        73.8,
        283.49999999999994,
        83.7
      ]
    },
    {
      "line_id": "l0007",
      "text": "Criminal law is the most direct expression of the relationship between a state and its citizens.",
      "bbox": [
        72.3,
        96.0,
        522.0,
        109.5
      ]
    },
    {
      "line_id": "l0008",
      "text": "Criminal sanction is indeed the most coercive method of regulating an individual’s behaviour",
      "bbox": [
        72.3,
        111.0,
        522.9,
        122.1
      ]
    },
    {
      "line_id": "l0009",
      "text": "which any state may deploy. The degree of coercion under criminal law is qualitatively",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        124.8,
        523.8,
        135.9
      ]
    },
    {
      "line_id": "l0010",
      "text": "different from the outcome in a dispute under civil law. The purpose of criminal law is to",
      "bbox": [
        72.3,
        138.6,
        522.6,
        149.7
      ]
    },
    {
      "line_id": "l0011",
      "text": "forbid and prevent conduct that unjustifiably and inexcusably inflicts or threatens substantial",
      "bbox": [
        72.3,
        152.7,
        522.6,
        163.79999999999998
      ]
    },
    {
      "line_id": "l0012",
      "text": "harm to individual or public interests. Feinberg explains the harm principle in following",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        166.2,
        522.9,
        177.29999999999998
      ]
    },
    {
      "line_id": "l0013",
      "text": "words: It is always a good reason in support of penal legislation that it would probably be",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        180.0,
        522.3,
        191.1
      ]
    },
    {
      "line_id": "l0014",
      "text": "effective in preventing (eliminating, reducing) harm to persons other than the actor and there",
      "bbox": [
        72.3,
        193.79999999999998,
        522.3,
        204.89999999999998
      ]
    },
    {
      "line_id": "l0015",
      "text": "is probably no other means that is equally effective at no greater cost to other values'.",
      "bbox": [
        72.3,
        207.6,
        480.3,
        218.7
      ]
    }
  ],
  "text": "90. Positivists were romanticists because:\na) They were running away from the realities of post-industrial Britain.\nb) They not imagining a perfectly ordered society.\nwere\nc) They were depicting the state of law and order of contemporary Britain.\nd) None of the above.\nCORRECT ANSWER : OPTION A\nCriminal law is the most direct expression of the relationship between a state and its citizens.\nCriminal sanction is indeed the most coercive method of regulating an individual’s behaviour\nwhich any state may deploy. The degree of coercion under criminal law is qualitatively\ndifferent from the outcome in a dispute under civil law. The purpose of criminal law is to\nforbid and prevent conduct that unjustifiably and inexcusably inflicts or threatens substantial\nharm to individual or public interests. Feinberg explains the harm principle in following\nwords: It is always a good reason in support of penal legislation that it would probably be\neffective in preventing (eliminating, reducing) harm to persons other than the actor and there\nis probably no other means that is equally effective at no greater cost to other values'.",
